  "generated_text": "First, simplify the expression inside the logarithm:\n\\[\n216\\sqrt{6} = 216 \\cdot 6^{1/2} = 216 \\cdot 6^{1/2} = 6^3 \\cdot 6^{1/2} = 6^{3 + 1/2} = 6^{7/2}\n\\]\n\nNext, rewrite the logarithm using the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_{\\sqrt{6}} (216\\sqrt{6}) = \\log_{6^{1/2}} (6^{7/2}) = \\frac{\\log (6^{7/2})}{\\log (6^{1/2})}\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log (6^{7/2})}{\\log (6^{1/2})} = \\frac{(7/2) \\log 6}{1/2 \\log 6} = \\frac{7/2}{1/2} = \\frac{7/2}{1} = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[7, '7']",
    "gold_answer": "Let $x= \\log_{\\sqrt{6}}(216\\sqrt{6})$.  Putting this in exponential notation gives $(\\sqrt{6})^x = 216\\sqrt{6}$.  Writing both sides with $6$ as the base gives us $6^{\\frac{x}{2}} = 6^3\\cdot 6^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6^{\\frac{7}{2}}$, so $x/2=7/2$.  Therefore, $x=\\boxed{7}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
